{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016qcfaH3XgF9FLeq5fWbRMN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, so RunDiffTests compares stdout, stderr, AND exit code (line 178-180). This means my stderr error messages need to match GNU printf exactly, OR I need to use a normalizer.\n\nLooking at line 178-180:\n```go\nstdoutMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stdout, refRes.stdout)\nstderrMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stderr, refRes.stderr)\nexitMatch := goRes.exitCode == refRes.exitCode\n```\n\nAll three must match! So my stderr output needs to match gprintf's stderr output. But gprintf's stderr messages use \"gprintf:\" as the prefix while my binary would use the binary path. \n\nI need to use a normalizer to strip or normalize the program name prefix from stderr. Looking at the echo test, none of the test cases produce errors, so stderr comparison is trivially matching since both are empty. For printf, error cases will produce stderr with different program names. Since the task is R1.1-R1.4, I should focus on basic functionality first—most tests should succeed without errors, and detailed error handling can come later.\n\nFor R1 scope, I'm concentrating on basic format string interpretation, integer and float specifiers working correctly, and string/char/%b handling. Error cases will produce stderr that needs to match, so I need a normalizer to replace the program name prefix with a consistent value. Writing a normalizer function... Writing normalizer function...\n\nFor R1, I'll skip error cases entirely and focus on the happy path with format specifiers. I should also handle the edge case where main is called without a format argument, which GNU printf treats as a missing operand error, but I'll defer that to later iterations and keep R1 focused on the core functionality. Writing the main package... Writing the escape and format processing... Writing escape sequence handlers... Writing parsing functions... Writi